WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.479 --> 00:00:11.479
Their playground, the Pacific Ocean.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.879
Lording it over such a vast kingdom,
they cut an all-too-familiar silhouette.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.600
Their popular reputation
as fierce predators is understandable.

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:35.680
Yet, what are they
but common scavengers?

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.919
The humble dustmen of the seas,

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:48.119
and all too often,
outsmarted by their juniors.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.159
Here are sharks in their true colors.

00:00:57.759 --> 00:01:01.200
The Pacific is by far
the largest body of water on the planet.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.319
Covering 70 million square miles,

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:07.120
it accounts
for half the world's ocean surface.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.720
Lost in this blue kingdom,
pinpricks of land where young seabirds

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.120
are about to leave their nests
for the first time.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:20.599
A fact not lost on life below the reef
that encircles the tiny islands

00:01:20.919 --> 00:01:24.120
of the French Frigate Shoals
in northwestern Hawaii.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.040
After weeks of training,

00:01:29.199 --> 00:01:32.000
black-footed albatross chicks
are ready for the big day.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.400
Their parents have already left
the nesting ground.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.319
Even the strong wind
blowing off the lagoon

00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:44.760
can't help this one.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.919
It's fair game for the most opportunistic
predator in the lagoon,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:51.639
the tiger shark.

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.440
It's both unpredictable
and extremely aggressive.

00:02:00.559 --> 00:02:04.879
Unlike other species of sharks out to sea,
the tiger shark is generally a loner

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.720
whose favorite hunting grounds
are the shallows.

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:13.360
Nothing like a nudge
in the right direction to get airborne.

00:02:34.399 --> 00:02:35.639
It wasn't to be.

00:02:37.759 --> 00:02:40.360
The chicks will try to take off in unison.

00:02:41.039 --> 00:02:44.440
So the tiger shark
only has a narrow window of opportunity

00:02:44.759 --> 00:02:49.240
for playing, feasting,
and saving its awesome reputation.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.160
In the open sea, sharks and birds
behave in an altogether different way.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.160
Every kind of ocean nomad
is drawn by the strong winds and currents

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.080
that galvanize the sea
into a maelstrom of competing interests,

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.039
both above and below the surface.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.440
How are these pristine opal waters
managed, or serviced, if you like?

00:03:58.399 --> 00:04:00.960
Like the tiger shark
policing the shallows alone,

00:04:01.399 --> 00:04:04.880
nomads out here work together
towards a common objective,

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.520
be it for defense or attack.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.040
Schools of baitfish
bunch together for protection.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.000
They're being rounded up
by a pod of bottlenose dolphins.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.799
The table is laid.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:50.440
Wait a moment.

00:04:50.559 --> 00:04:52.359
Unexpected guests have shown up,

00:04:52.799 --> 00:04:54.959
and the dolphins must leave the table.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.720
Silky sharks.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.320
They've been watching the dolphins
work from a distance.

00:05:08.559 --> 00:05:11.480
Their vision finely attuned
to the blue gloom

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.959
because of special pigments in their eyes.

00:05:34.559 --> 00:05:37.600
The dolphins sensibly wait
for a second sitting,

00:05:38.679 --> 00:05:41.239
which they'll share
with others on the surface.

00:05:42.279 --> 00:05:45.600
Gannets and brown petrels
enjoy a bird's-eye view,

00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:48.200
but their patience is wearing thin.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:13.480
Now that the waters have been tested,
a full aerial bombardment is underway.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.760
These feathered torpedoes
must climb to ten meters

00:06:25.839 --> 00:06:28.440
before executing
their spectacular sorties.

00:06:29.119 --> 00:06:33.760
This extraordinary assault by shark
and bird against the bewildered baitfish

00:06:33.959 --> 00:06:35.119
is relentless.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.239
Having taken the lion's share,

00:06:42.519 --> 00:06:45.959
the silky sharks withdraw
as willfully as they came.

00:06:49.359 --> 00:06:51.799
It's a moment of respite for the baitfish,

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:57.760
but not for long.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:03.320
Yellowfin tuna form a third front,
wave after wave of them.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.440
This is predation on a massive scale.

00:07:08.559 --> 00:07:11.399
The remaining baitfish
have one last card to play.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:15.000
They swarm individual silky sharks,
not for revenge,

00:07:15.279 --> 00:07:17.320
but for protection against the tuna.

00:07:31.559 --> 00:07:33.279
The baitfish face a dilemma.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:37.279
Should they jump ship from shark to tuna?

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:51.399
The silence of this predatory world
speaks for itself.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.359
There are areas of the Pacific
where natural predators

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:05.440
turn to scavenging for a living.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.600
The main island in the archipelago
of New Caledonia

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:17.679
is a sliver of land in the South Pacific,
250 miles long and 30 wide.

00:08:19.679 --> 00:08:22.239
Smaller islands bathe
in the crystal clear waters

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:23.480
of a tropical lagoon.

00:08:24.119 --> 00:08:28.239
The largest lagoon in the world,
9,000 square miles.

00:08:28.679 --> 00:08:32.440
It's a paradise meticulously tended
by those who inhabit it.

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.320
No paradise is one without sacrifice.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.559
Those who share it
share off each other too.

00:08:41.599 --> 00:08:45.440
Everywhere life thrives,
death will reap a just reward.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.400
The lagoon creates waste
and must dispose of it.

00:08:58.599 --> 00:09:02.440
One day, the dead and the wounded
will be mopped up by scavengers.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:04.679
The dustmen of the seas.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:24.479
The lagoon is not impenetrable.

00:09:24.919 --> 00:09:27.679
Cracks are opened up
in this vast ring of coral

00:09:28.039 --> 00:09:30.080
by the relentless pounding of the ocean.

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:57.840
The whitetip reef shark
is an expert dam buster.

00:10:00.479 --> 00:10:02.760
A red snapper snared in a net.

00:10:03.119 --> 00:10:05.679
The shark hones in on the distress signals

00:10:05.799 --> 00:10:08.400
as the snapper emits
a faint electrical charge,

00:10:08.559 --> 00:10:09.919
even when it's motionless.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:19.799
Literally a millionth of a volt
is enough to stimulate sensory organs

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:23.159
called ampullae of Lorenzini
on the shark's snout.

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:28.119
In effect, the injured and the weak
sign their own death warrant.

00:11:00.159 --> 00:11:03.440
A grey reef shark approaches
the industrious whitetips

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:06.080
keen to join operation cleanup.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:18.640
Its impressive size does not earn it
the automatic right to muscle in.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.760
This is a private party
for whitetips only.

00:11:29.359 --> 00:11:32.960
Sharks themselves also run the risk
of getting caught in nets,

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:36.280
particularly those more used
to inhabiting the open sea.

00:11:40.599 --> 00:11:44.080
But white tip reef sharks are equipped
with two rows of teeth.

00:11:44.799 --> 00:11:47.359
The uppers are triangulated and jagged.

00:11:48.239 --> 00:11:50.719
The lower row, narrow and sharp.

00:11:53.239 --> 00:11:56.719
A perfect combination for cutting
and tearing their way out of trouble.

00:11:58.039 --> 00:12:00.880
Damaged teeth will be replaced in 18 days,

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:05.200
and they'll grow about 20,000 new teeth
in the course of their lives.

00:12:06.799 --> 00:12:10.200
As long as whitetip reef shark
move freely and hunt in packs,

00:12:10.599 --> 00:12:13.599
neither nets nor other sharks
will stop them in their mission

00:12:14.719 --> 00:12:17.359
to clean up the lagoon
of injured and weak fish.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:30.159
Inadvertently, the sharks will even help
dismantle these destructive nets.

00:12:35.359 --> 00:12:37.479
This snapper might count itself lucky.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.679
If only it could hide its distress.

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:46.880
The more it struggles to stay afloat,
the stronger the signal.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:02.599
Thanks to the shark's natural radar

00:13:02.799 --> 00:13:07.359
and its capacity to detect vibrations
from far, the area is cleared.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:13.880
Pockets of coral and limestone caves
do offer some insulation from this radar,

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:17.119
which, it must be said,
is only used at short range.

00:13:18.599 --> 00:13:20.520
But what is this one after?

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:41.640
A swimming wrasse
which emits long-range vibrations.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:14.799
Whitetips will go to great lengths
to satisfy their appetite,

00:14:15.320 --> 00:14:17.039
or is it just curiosity?

00:14:24.799 --> 00:14:27.679
After all,
they need only feed every other day

00:14:27.799 --> 00:14:29.960
and a mere three percent
of their body weight

00:14:30.039 --> 00:14:31.799
is reckoned a good enough meal.

00:14:44.479 --> 00:14:48.200
Some fetch up at the mouth of a cave
through a break in the reef,

00:14:48.719 --> 00:14:50.320
a good place for an ambush.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:52.919
Unlike their cousins in the open water,

00:14:53.159 --> 00:14:56.039
whitetips don't need
to be on the move to breathe.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:09.119
They can lie and wait for their prey
by using muscular contractions

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:11.280
to pump water through their gills.

00:15:27.359 --> 00:15:30.599
These males
aren't hungry for food, but sex.

00:15:31.159 --> 00:15:32.640
They're waiting for a female.

00:15:36.119 --> 00:15:38.960
She's hemmed in
on all sides by competing males,

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:42.640
but the paramour
must fend off other excited rivals.

00:16:24.479 --> 00:16:27.000
The lovemaking
will last about five minutes,

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:31.359
during which time the suitor must fend off
other rivals drawn into the scene

00:16:31.679 --> 00:16:35.640
by the vibrations and electrical impulses
radiating from the courtship.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:50.640
The female probably got off lightly.

00:16:53.559 --> 00:16:55.719
This one is unlikely to survive.

00:17:01.479 --> 00:17:04.560
Her blood involuntarily signals
her vulnerable condition

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:06.760
to every pilferer in the area,

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:12.920
including the biggest fish in the world,

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:17.319
the whale shark.

00:17:24.239 --> 00:17:28.920
It's 15 meters long, and quite harmless.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:35.199
Like real whales,
the whale shark is a filterer

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:38.599
using its cavernous jaws
to hoover up plankton

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.959
and other small fry in its path.

00:17:47.439 --> 00:17:49.560
It too, is a most efficient cleaner,

00:17:49.839 --> 00:17:52.719
though quite incapable
of cleaning up a dying shark.

00:17:57.079 --> 00:17:59.680
Commotion, blood, electricity.

00:18:00.359 --> 00:18:03.839
The telltale distress signals
radiate loud and clear.

00:18:08.359 --> 00:18:11.400
This silvertip has homed in
from the bottom of the pass.

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:23.680
A welcoming committee is awaiting.

00:18:24.199 --> 00:18:25.680
The red snappers are back,

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:35.040
and so are the grey reef sharks.

00:19:32.800 --> 00:19:35.800
These passing bottlenose dolphins
and their calves

00:19:36.079 --> 00:19:37.839
steer clear of the danger.

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:43.520
A silvertip this size
is also a potential threat

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:44.920
to baby dolphins.

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:57.119
The shark must be seen off.

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:15.160
Indeed, the dolphins take it in turns
to go up to the surface to breathe.

00:20:24.479 --> 00:20:26.000
The tactic is ingenious.

00:20:35.599 --> 00:20:40.599
The dolphins spiral around the silvertip
to force him down out of harm's way.

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:06.560
Reinforcements are called in
to increase the pressure.

00:21:12.719 --> 00:21:17.000
It's now five against one
and the silvertip finally gets the point.

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:23.800
The dolphins can't disguise their delight.

00:21:24.959 --> 00:21:27.079
It's time to surface and celebrate.

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:42.719
The pass through the reef is now clear

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:45.319
and the dolphins are free
to enter the shallows

00:21:45.599 --> 00:21:48.239
and explore the waters
around the sandy islands.

00:21:58.479 --> 00:22:01.479
As spring turns to summer
in the Southern Hemisphere,

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:05.359
a time-honored pilgrimage
is about to wash up on these shores.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.160
Same time, same place.

00:22:10.599 --> 00:22:13.760
A green turtle scarred by a long journey.

00:22:21.959 --> 00:22:24.119
The healthy ones
will perpetuate the species

00:22:24.199 --> 00:22:26.599
as they have done for 15 million years.

00:22:27.319 --> 00:22:31.160
An annual rendezvous for mating
and for the females to lay eggs.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:35.520
The event is also on the calendar
of the blacktip shark.

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:47.760
At high tide, it watches greedily
as the females prepare to beach.

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:50.680
The solitary turtles
have crossed the Pacific

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:52.199
with pinpoint accuracy.

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:56.079
Thanks, it is thought,
to some magnetic minerals in their brain

00:22:56.479 --> 00:22:59.359
which tap into the Earth's
magnetic field for direction.

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:04.239
The tide fills natural pools
around the beachrock,

00:23:04.479 --> 00:23:06.560
allowing the sharks to get close to shore.

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:16.640
A female, all 80 pounds of her,
hauls her eggs

00:23:16.719 --> 00:23:20.280
far from the high water mark,
more than a kilometer inland.

00:23:25.479 --> 00:23:27.359
The sharks know the routine.

00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:31.239
They'll patrol the pools
until the tide flushes them out again.

00:23:41.719 --> 00:23:44.959
The turtles have 12 hours
to dig and fill their nests

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:47.359
before catching the next high tide out.

00:23:50.359 --> 00:23:51.680
As the tide goes out,

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.839
sand crabs also dig in
for a night of hunting.

00:24:03.599 --> 00:24:07.280
As night falls,
blacktips still brush the shoreline.

00:24:14.719 --> 00:24:17.800
Grey reef sharks
patrol further out at sea.

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:19.719
A blockade is on.

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:34.239
With the tide now low,

00:24:34.359 --> 00:24:36.719
some turtles have finished
laying their eggs.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:39.839
A blanket of sand will keep them
at a constant temperature

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:43.439
and hide them from predators
during a two-month period of incubation.

00:24:50.239 --> 00:24:53.400
The females now begin
the arduous return journey.

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:58.439
By morning,
most have left on the high tide.

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:03.439
Those that have missed it must face
a punishing wall of exposed coral

00:25:03.520 --> 00:25:04.760
under the baking sun.

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:07.520
All after an exhausting night's work.

00:25:12.479 --> 00:25:14.520
It's the end of the road for this one.

00:25:21.359 --> 00:25:25.319
Her carcass now reeks
of rich oils and torn flesh.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:30.400
On cue, gray reefs
and blacktips follow their noses.

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:32.959
To locate dead prey,

00:25:33.119 --> 00:25:35.839
these scavengers are guided
by their sense of smell.

00:26:07.439 --> 00:26:10.920
Reclusive hermit crabs
are left to polish the silver.

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:50.199
By mid-summer, the dry season has set in
on the main island of New Caledonia.

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:59.640
A mountain range on the Leeward Coast
creates long periods of drought.

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:03.199
It's home
to tens of thousands of wild deer

00:27:03.599 --> 00:27:05.959
who now look elsewhere for green pasture.

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:17.319
Some head for the mangroves.

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:29.640
Many will leave the island altogether

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:33.719
and venture across the bay
in search of food on other islands.

00:27:46.599 --> 00:27:49.400
Their narrow hooves
are ill-suited for swimming.

00:27:54.640 --> 00:27:58.280
Hopefully, the natural buoyancy
of their paunch will keep them afloat.

00:27:59.199 --> 00:28:01.800
The deer can average two knots
over long distances,

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:05.040
simply by repeating
the same movements they use on land.

00:28:07.160 --> 00:28:11.719
Across the lagoon in any direction,
lie dozens of tiny uninhabited islands.

00:28:12.239 --> 00:28:15.680
They're thick with grasses
and foliage supplied all year-round

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:18.880
by soft water
trapped in the limestone subsoil.

00:28:20.359 --> 00:28:23.959
The crossing has been worthwhile,
even if some of the deer have struggled

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:27.599
two miles from the mainland
to find new feeding grounds.

00:28:31.280 --> 00:28:35.040
They'll spend the entire summer
island hopping to survive the drought.

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:41.079
Every day brings new arrivals.

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:53.040
This kid is exhausted,
but at least he's made it.

00:29:19.959 --> 00:29:21.959
Many die in midstream.

00:29:25.599 --> 00:29:29.040
Waste to be disposed of
by the dustmen of the seas.

00:29:34.119 --> 00:29:37.640
A mere one part blood
to 100 million parts water

00:29:37.959 --> 00:29:39.800
is enough to trigger a frenzy.

00:30:09.599 --> 00:30:10.839
By the end of the summer,

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:13.920
the last of the nesting turtles
are leaving the islands.

00:30:26.359 --> 00:30:28.560
Hundreds of eggs are ready to hatch.

00:31:03.680 --> 00:31:06.920
Instinctively, the baby turtles
head for the pool.

00:32:01.959 --> 00:32:03.880
There's no scent of blood this time.

00:32:04.439 --> 00:32:08.880
Here, the blacktips must use their eyes,
if their patience is to be rewarded.

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:13.040
This is a problem
because they're short-sighted

00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:15.800
and their movements are cramped
by the shallow waters.

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:17.640
In the event, the sharks are joined

00:33:17.719 --> 00:33:20.920
by other, faster
and more efficient predators.

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:27.479
Giant trevally.

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:30.040
More commonly known as Jack.

00:33:30.680 --> 00:33:33.640
Pound for pound,
it's the strongest fish in the world,

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:35.560
sharks included.

00:33:36.680 --> 00:33:41.319
It's perfectly suited to the crystal clear
and well-oxygenated shallows.

00:33:42.359 --> 00:33:43.839
This is the proof.

00:34:52.479 --> 00:34:56.080
By contrast, it takes two myopic
and clumsy blacktips

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:58.360
to hunt down a single baby turtle.

00:35:37.120 --> 00:35:39.719
Those which survive
the blockade to the open sea

00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:42.600
must now contend
with predators from the sky.

00:35:43.959 --> 00:35:46.959
What with these terns,
sharks, and the jacks,

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:51.840
it's little wonder that out of 100 eggs,
only one turtle will survive to adulthood.

00:36:00.639 --> 00:36:01.840
If this is one of them,

00:36:02.159 --> 00:36:05.840
it will be back
in 15 years to mate or lay eggs.

00:36:06.399 --> 00:36:08.959
The scavengers will make a point
of being there too.

00:36:10.120 --> 00:36:13.719
For the most part, life about the lagoon
is entirely predictable.

00:36:18.639 --> 00:36:20.560
Here is a magnificent exception.

00:36:24.439 --> 00:36:26.560
An 18-meter blue whale.

00:36:27.159 --> 00:36:29.560
The largest animal
ever to roam the planet.

00:36:30.439 --> 00:36:32.080
At the end of the southern summer,

00:36:32.159 --> 00:36:35.120
this male should be feeding
on krill in the Antarctic,

00:36:35.959 --> 00:36:39.760
but it's lost its way
and mysteriously fetched up in the lagoon.

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:43.479
Never has this been seen before
in New Caledonia.

00:36:47.719 --> 00:36:50.800
Stretch marks are signs
of severe undernourishment.

00:36:53.639 --> 00:36:59.080
If the lagoon can't satisfy its needs,
perhaps it can satisfy others'.

00:37:05.439 --> 00:37:10.560
Forty tons of flesh
emit rapid, low frequency sound waves.

00:37:12.199 --> 00:37:15.560
The tiger shark has picked up the signal
from several miles away

00:37:15.919 --> 00:37:20.679
as the whale reaches the bottom
of the bay, exhausted and cornered.

00:38:37.679 --> 00:38:40.159
In the end, there is only one killer.

00:38:40.919 --> 00:38:42.000
What a treat.

00:38:42.479 --> 00:38:46.439
Rare indeed for this tiger shark
to have honed in on so much red meat.

00:38:47.679 --> 00:38:49.360
It has pushed itself to the limit

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:52.280
and has swallowed a tenth
of its own body weight.

00:39:06.439 --> 00:39:10.439
Other sharks were probably too far off
in the lagoon to make it in time,

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:20.919
but the blue whale is going no further.

00:39:36.080 --> 00:39:41.040
Day after day, the whale's corpse
will radiate a gigantic, oily path.

00:39:41.800 --> 00:39:45.360
Sharks and their stereoscopic sense
of smell will simply follow

00:39:45.439 --> 00:39:47.000
this stairway to heaven.

00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:58.919
This treasure of protein has been bagged
by a whole pack of tigers,

00:39:59.159 --> 00:40:01.879
and they won't let
any other species of shark near it.

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:08.040
Only a few harmless remoras are tolerated.

00:40:27.320 --> 00:40:29.320
The final cleanup is underway.

00:42:11.360 --> 00:42:15.719
It has taken a week and counting
to clear all 18 tons of meat.

00:42:16.360 --> 00:42:18.719
What little evidence is left
of the blue whale

00:42:18.800 --> 00:42:22.280
will filter through passes in the lagoon
for the final clean

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:24.800
in the dark abyss of the open sea.

00:42:39.679 --> 00:42:43.080
About 85 percent of the Pacific
is more than a mile deep.

00:42:43.959 --> 00:42:46.439
Plenty of room for other,
more passive scavengers

00:42:46.679 --> 00:42:50.159
waiting in the dark and cold
and under colossal pressure

00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:53.159
for the pickings left
by their peers on the surface.

00:42:55.520 --> 00:42:58.280
Down here, life exists without oxygen.

00:43:03.080 --> 00:43:07.080
Deep sea hydrothermal vents
sustain an oasis of life.

00:43:09.439 --> 00:43:12.840
Located on the side of a fault
between two tectonic plates,

00:43:13.239 --> 00:43:16.159
New Caledonia
is effectively a mountain in the ocean.

00:43:17.080 --> 00:43:22.159
These gigantic fractures in the crust
belch gases through seafloor chimneys.

00:43:23.679 --> 00:43:26.679
In this anaerobic and unstable underworld,

00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:30.840
life is organized
around methane and hydrogen sulfide,

00:43:31.199 --> 00:43:35.159
highly toxic on land,
but life-sustaining in the abyss.

00:43:37.199 --> 00:43:41.520
At the bottom of the evolutionary pile
in this ecosystem is the tube worm.

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:45.639
It has no mouth,
no digestive tract, and no anus.

00:43:46.560 --> 00:43:49.080
Everything is passed through its plumes.

00:43:52.080 --> 00:43:56.439
The thermal vents superheat the water
to 350 degrees Celsius,

00:43:56.959 --> 00:44:00.320
but at depths of 3,000 meters,
the water does not boil.

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:02.239
The pressure is too great.

00:44:05.560 --> 00:44:08.040
So blind shrimps are free
to scrape the vents

00:44:08.120 --> 00:44:09.919
for minerals and bacteria.

00:44:12.199 --> 00:44:15.399
It's remarkable
how marine life can adapt to this hell.

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:22.080
Away from the vents, notably at the foot
of the New Caledonian lagoon,

00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:23.439
there is nothing.

00:44:24.080 --> 00:44:27.040
Life in these abyssal deserts
depends essentially

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:29.239
on what sinks from the surface.

00:44:31.199 --> 00:44:33.639
When scavengers of the dark
reach their objective,

00:44:34.000 --> 00:44:36.560
relationships can be
extremely long-lasting.

00:44:38.120 --> 00:44:41.919
Giant crabs will take several years
to pick a whale carcass clean.

00:44:54.639 --> 00:44:57.159
Difficult to be spotted
in this dark world,

00:44:57.560 --> 00:45:01.199
unless like some squid,
you are equipped with photophores,

00:45:01.760 --> 00:45:03.360
a sort of light-producing organ.

00:45:04.120 --> 00:45:07.760
They use them to attract prey
or to seduce sexual partners.

00:45:08.479 --> 00:45:11.919
These luminous cells
can also be seen on dying animals.

00:45:21.520 --> 00:45:25.239
Others must grope their way
around the ocean floor in their search

00:45:25.320 --> 00:45:28.280
for organic matter, dead or alive.

00:45:30.439 --> 00:45:33.239
It's a task made easier
for the birdbeak dogfish,

00:45:33.679 --> 00:45:38.120
and like its cousins from the lagoon,
its stereoscopic sense of smell.

00:45:43.840 --> 00:45:47.239
Other scavengers must wait
for the currents to carry food to them.

00:45:47.679 --> 00:45:49.199
Like this tripod fish.

00:45:52.159 --> 00:45:56.399
They can also filter organic particles
like this swimming sea cucumber

00:45:56.800 --> 00:45:58.159
and these crinoids.

00:46:04.719 --> 00:46:08.560
The vampire squid uses sensory cilia
to detect what's edible

00:46:08.679 --> 00:46:10.080
in the ocean swells.

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:19.719
What do we have here?

00:46:23.399 --> 00:46:25.639
The most prized dish in the abyss.

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:27.040
A whole whale.

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:30.399
It's covered with hagfish.

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:34.760
These living fossils have been around
for five million years,

00:46:35.239 --> 00:46:36.919
long before any other fish.

00:46:39.159 --> 00:46:45.959
They're vertebrates with no eyes,
no skeleton, no scales, not even a mouth.

00:46:47.040 --> 00:46:51.199
They do have an external circle of teeth
and a strong sense of smell.

00:46:52.719 --> 00:46:55.840
But a million hagfish can't match this.

00:46:56.679 --> 00:46:58.719
The Pacific sleeper shark,

00:46:59.320 --> 00:47:03.679
indolent and sluggish by nature,
but very greedy.

00:47:20.600 --> 00:47:25.959
Seven meters long and weighing two tons,
it's the largest of all deep water fish

00:47:26.679 --> 00:47:30.159
and it has an insatiable desire
to keep a 300-liter stomach

00:47:30.520 --> 00:47:31.719
comfortably full.

00:47:54.639 --> 00:47:58.919
Most life around the lagoon
in New Caledonia ends up in the abyss.

00:47:59.679 --> 00:48:03.320
Even the oceanic whitetip sharks
are destined to become a meal

00:48:03.399 --> 00:48:05.040
for the galatheid crabs.

00:48:07.239 --> 00:48:09.320
One dustman feeds off another.

00:48:10.159 --> 00:48:13.280
A fitting end
for the scavengers of the seas.

